<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manila is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, on Manila Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. The Spanish established it as the colonial capital of the Philippines in 1571. It became the Asian terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> trade, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and making Manila a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s Pacific commerce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manila.jpg" alt="Manila" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bahnfrend · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cityscape_of_Manila,_2025_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brunei tradition credits the fifth sultan, Bolkiah, remembered as Nakhoda Ragam, the 'singing captain', with expeditions that extended Brunei's influence over the coasts of Borneo, Sulu and the <b>Manila</b> region in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish silver dollar, or piece of eight, circulated from Europe to China, especially after the Manila galleons began sailing between Acapulco and <b>Manila</b> in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver imported from Japan and, via <b>Manila</b>, from Spanish America monetised the economy and fed population growth, urbanisation and commercial expansion, especially in the Yangtze delta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manila galleons sailed between Acapulco and <b>Manila</b> for 250 years, from 1565 to 1815, carrying Mexican silver to Asia and returning with Chinese silk and porcelain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1565 to 1815 the Manila Galleon linked Acapulco and <b>Manila</b>, the first sustained direct trade link between the Americas and Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish, established at <b>Manila</b> from 1571, launched repeated expeditions in the long conflicts they called the Moro wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
